<%- include("partials/head") %>
<%- include("partials/header") %>
<%- include("partials/nav") %>

<main class="resume-layout">
  <%- include("partials/contactModal") %>

  <article class="project-detail">
    <header class="card project-detail-hero">
      <a href="/projects" class="project-back-link">Back to projects</a>
      <p class="project-tag">Backend API · Python / FastAPI / PostgreSQL</p>
      <h1>Board Game Intelligence API</h1>
      <p>
        A backend and data integration API that turns BoardGameGeek-style XML
        data into validated relational records, tracks import history and
        exposes documented endpoints for game browsing and recommendation
        reports.
      </p>

      <div class="project-detail-actions">
        <a
          href="https://board-game-intelligence-api.onrender.com/docs"
          class="project-link project-link-primary"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          API Docs
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/STU6266/board-game-intelligence-api"
          class="project-link project-link-secondary"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          GitHub
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="project-detail-media-grid" aria-label="Board Game Intelligence API visuals">
      <figure class="project-media-card">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/board-game-api/swagger-docs.png"
          alt="Swagger documentation page showing Board Game Intelligence API endpoints"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Live Swagger/OpenAPI documentation</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="project-media-card">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/board-game-api/family-friendly-response.png"
          alt="JSON response from the family-friendly board game report endpoint"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Example API response from a report endpoint</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="project-media-card">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/board-game-api/architecture-flow.png"
          alt="Architecture diagram showing XML data, parsing, validation, PostgreSQL, FastAPI and reports"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Architecture flow based on the project README</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-grid">
      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          I built this project to show backend work beyond simple CRUD. The
          important part is the data pipeline: fetch or load external XML,
          parse it, validate it, synchronize it into normalized tables and make
          the result explorable through a documented API.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Tech Stack</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Python 3.12, FastAPI, Pydantic</li>
          <li>PostgreSQL, SQLAlchemy, Alembic</li>
          <li>HTTPX, Docker Compose, Pytest</li>
          <li>Swagger/OpenAPI documentation, Render deployment</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Features</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>BoardGameGeek-style XML parsing into internal data structures</li>
          <li>Data quality checks before records are saved</li>
          <li>Update-by-BGG-ID logic to avoid duplicate games</li>
          <li>Import-run and import-error tracking for reviewable sync results</li>
          <li>Analytical report endpoints for family-friendly and accessible games</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Architecture</h2>
        <p>
          The API is split into endpoints, schemas, SQLAlchemy models,
          repositories and service classes. Parsing, validation, synchronization
          and reporting stay in service logic, while repositories handle
          database access. That separation keeps API handlers small and makes
          the important backend behavior easier to test.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Challenges</h2>
        <p>
          The hardest part was treating external data as unreliable input.
          Player counts, playtime, ratings and optional fields need validation
          before storage. Failed imports are stored as data, so problems can be
          inspected later instead of disappearing into console output.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>What I Learned</h2>
        <p>
          This project helped me understand how production-style APIs are built:
          clear layers, migrations, environment-based configuration, tests for
          external integrations and defensive data handling before information
          reaches the database.
        </p>
      </section>
    </section>
  </article>
</main>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
